<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bacan Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1950 CE (approx.) · capital Bacan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southernmost of the four kingdoms of the North Moluccas, on the islands of Bacan south of Halmahera.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bacan was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moloku-kie-raha">Moloku Kie Raha</a>, the four Moluccan kingdoms with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jailolo-sultanate">Jailolo</a>. Its rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the late 15th century, like their neighbours, as Javanese and Malay traders sought Moluccan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>. Local tradition traces the four royal houses to a common ancestor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bacan was smaller and poorer than <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore</a> but grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a> and controlled islands to the south and the Obi group. In 1557 its sultan accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> under Portuguese influence, though later rulers were Muslim. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-barneveld">Fort Barneveld</a> on Bacan in the early 17th century, and the sultanate was often a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Ternate.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bacan came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control in the 19th century and its sultan became a colonial client. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a> the sultanate was absorbed into the new state's administration around 1950; the royal family keeps a ceremonial role in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-maluku">North Maluku</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bacan-sultanate.jpg" alt="Bacan Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayyubid_Flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bacan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bacan Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Bacan, Batjan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=bacan-sultanate" aria-label="Bacan Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7506 2217 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bacan Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8538 2404h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7826 2473h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7826 2473h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=bacan-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
